{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>And from there he saw the edge of the people.</b> For the edges are most susceptible to harm, as it is said of the complainers (Numbers 11:1) ״and it consumed the edge of the people״ — whether at the first edge, the chiefs of the people, or at the last edge, the outcasts among the people. For from the side of the outcasts they would be truly fit for curse, and if from the side of the important chiefs — it is easy to bring the evil eye upon them because of their excellence, for this is the path that Balaam chose for himself: if he finds no ground to curse them, he will bring the evil eye upon them." "And you, the discerning reader, lift up your eyes and see the variations that we find in three places. For first it is stated: ״And from there he saw the edge of the people״; and afterward it is stated: ״only the edge of it you shall see, and the whole of it you shall not see״ — the inference being that at the first edge he saw the whole of it through seeing the edge; and in the third place it is stated: ״and he saw Israel dwelling by its tribes״ — meaning that he saw all of them literally. And the explanation of this matter is that first Balaam peered at the root of Israel — that is, their source and the rock from which they were hewn — whether he might find in it a sin through which the curse would take hold also upon the branches, for when the root is nullified the branches too fall. And this is seeing the edge that encompasses them all, for the Patriarchs include the children. And after he sought and did not find, he turned back to peer at the branches — whether he might find sin in the descendants. And this is the edge whose whole he did not see, for the Patriarchs are not included among the children. And after he found no iniquity, neither in the Patriarchs nor in the descendants, he said: from now on, they are all at the height of perfection; if so, I will bring upon them the evil eye, which holds greatest sway over a thing that is perfect from every side, in which there is no blemish whatsoever. As is concluded in the Tanḥuma (Balaq 15), and as the Yalkut brings on the verse ״one who blesses his fellow with a loud voice, early in the morning, it is counted as a curse״ (Proverbs 27:14) — concerning Balaam, who sought to bring the evil eye upon Israel through his blessings. And along this path I have seen fit to explain all three places, and this is their order." "<b>And he brought him up to the high places of Baal, and from there he saw the edge of the people.</b> This is the first investigation, in which he peered at the root of the nation. And he searched and found that at first our fathers were idol worshippers, as it is written (Joshua 24:2): ״Beyond the River your fathers dwelt of old — Terah the father of Abraham and the father of Nahor — and they served other gods״ — that is, the high places of Baal, for they went after the Baals, for their masters were lords other than Him, blessed be He. And from there he saw the edge of the people: the root is the first edge, and it is counted as the whole, for all the children branch off from the root. And when the root is fit for curse, surely it will take hold also upon the heads of the branches, for through idolatry He visits the iniquity of fathers upon children." "And prophecy came to Balaam that Balak and Balaam had both fallen into a great error, for the head of the nation's lineage is not from Aram. For Terah was considered as dead in his lifetime, so that Abraham was exempt from the commandment of honoring father and mother — hear from this that he is not included as the head of lineage of this nation. Rather, the beginning of this nation is from the peak of the rocks — this is Abraham. And this is what it says: ״And he took up his parable and said: From Aram Balak leads me״ — that is, he wishes to make the head of this nation Terah, who dwelt in Aram Naharaim, מֵרֹאשׁ הַרְרֵי קֶדֶם, that is, from Terah who preceded Abraham, and he saw him worshipping idolatry, against whom rises ״Cursed is the man who makes a carved image״ etc. (Deuteronomy 27:15). Therefore he sought ״Come, curse me Jacob״ — for surely the curse will take hold upon the lesser ones, as indicated by the name Jacob. ״And come, denounce Israel״ — for through this there will be wrath and anger even upon the good among them, who are alluded to in the name Israel, through their seeing the destruction of their kindred."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:52.006215537Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}